WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.885 --> 00:00:12.655
What do you think when you take
a look at these two images?

00:00:12.655 --> 00:00:15.136
Do you see any connection?

00:00:15.136 --> 00:00:18.356
Maybe, maybe not.

00:00:18.356 --> 00:00:20.586
We will talk about it later.

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:22.666
Now look at this picture.

00:00:22.666 --> 00:00:27.225
My dad keeps watching a lot
of movies of this man,

00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:34.489
and he has his own version of a very
famous dialogue from one of his movies:

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:37.527
"There are two kinds of people
in the world.

00:00:37.527 --> 00:00:41.327
The ones who create problems

00:00:41.327 --> 00:00:46.096
and the ones who solve them."

00:00:46.096 --> 00:00:48.877
I want to be in the latter category.

00:00:48.877 --> 00:00:53.067
Now, let me tell you what 
I really want to do,

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:59.444
besides showing simple 
correlational pictures.

00:00:59.444 --> 00:01:04.466
I want to solve a problem,
a very big problem.

00:01:04.466 --> 00:01:09.508
A 5-trillion-piece problem
that's plaguing us today:

00:01:09.508 --> 00:01:14.039
the problem of what we throw
into our oceans.

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:18.516
Did you know by 2050 
we will have the same, or maybe more,

00:01:18.516 --> 00:01:21.516
plastic in the ocean than fish?

00:01:21.516 --> 00:01:27.966
Or if we were to take all the plastic
on the ocean surface and pile it up -

00:01:27.966 --> 00:01:31.966
do you know how far 
a distance it would cover?

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:33.845
Take a guess.

00:01:33.845 --> 00:01:39.585
It would be enough to stack two-liter 
bottles from here to the moon and back

00:01:39.585 --> 00:01:42.326
twice.

00:01:42.326 --> 00:01:45.936
That's one long walk.

00:01:45.936 --> 00:01:50.777
To put into perspective 
the ocean pollution, as you can imagine,

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:54.986
is a very, very big problem.

00:01:54.986 --> 00:01:59.165
While these statistics are huge 
and mind-boggling,

00:01:59.165 --> 00:02:03.506
what really moved me was the impact
that this waste has on life.

00:02:03.506 --> 00:02:08.075
I learned something about whales recently.

00:02:08.075 --> 00:02:15.367
Only July 2013, this young whale ended
up on the shores of Northern Netherlands.

00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:20.819
The people were very surprised to find
an unusually bloated stomach.

00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:23.948
When they opened it up, they found,
among other things,

00:02:23.948 --> 00:02:26.629
two horse pipes,

00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:28.990
nine-meter rope,

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:33.870
and 37 pounds of plastic.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.210
It's cause of death?

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.568
Intestinal blockage.

00:02:38.568 --> 00:02:42.624
What emotions run within you
when you see this helpless seal

00:02:42.624 --> 00:02:46.260
entangled within this net?

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:52.039
Anger, pain, or perhaps even shame?

00:02:52.039 --> 00:02:55.319
There are many more 
horrifying pictures i have seen,

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:58.389
which I found extremely disturbing.

00:02:58.389 --> 00:03:01.139
Every time I see people eating fish,

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:05.248
I feel that we're not just feeding
plastic to fish,

00:03:05.248 --> 00:03:09.209
we are feeding it to ourselves as well.

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:12.789
The plastic we dispose,
which ends up in the ocean,

00:03:12.789 --> 00:03:19.468
gets consumed by small organisms
which are later eaten by bigger fish,

00:03:19.468 --> 00:03:23.408
which might just be the fish
that ends up on our plate.

00:03:23.408 --> 00:03:27.289
Talk about the vicious karmic cycle.

00:03:27.289 --> 00:03:32.727
All this while we are trying to find
water and life on other planets,

00:03:32.727 --> 00:03:35.109
and I think and smile.

00:03:35.109 --> 00:03:39.157
If we cannot do justice to life
and water on our planet,

00:03:39.157 --> 00:03:44.548
what are we trying to achieve
by searching it outside our Earth?

00:03:44.548 --> 00:03:47.028
With so much happening around us,

00:03:47.028 --> 00:03:50.599
it got a bit depressing
and overwhelming for me

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:55.530
that I felt I had to do 
something about it.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.699
Hence, I came up with an idea 
of an invention

00:03:58.699 --> 00:04:02.913
that could help make the oceans
a cleaner place:

00:04:02.913 --> 00:04:06.288
an ocean cleaner called ERVIS.

00:04:06.288 --> 00:04:10.779
Now getting back to the two pictures 
we saw in the beginning.

00:04:10.779 --> 00:04:17.489
Garbage plus the sink
equals to ERVIS.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:20.240
So what is ERVIS all about?

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.950
ERVIS is an intelligent ship which
sucks waste from the surface of the ocean

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:28.158
and cleans it.

00:04:28.158 --> 00:04:31.976
It all started last year when I saw
some documentaries

00:04:31.976 --> 00:04:36.011
on water pollution 
and how it is plaguing us.

00:04:36.011 --> 00:04:40.581
I also happened to see Boyan Slat's 
TED Talk on the same problem

00:04:40.581 --> 00:04:44.349
and how he wanted to tackle it.

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:47.815
Then one day, as I came home
from playing football,

00:04:47.815 --> 00:04:52.080
my mom asked me to wash my hands.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.108
And as I went to the sink
to get the dirt off,

00:04:55.108 --> 00:04:57.980
I saw how the water flowed
into the sink hole

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:02.967
and an idea came to my mind
and I smiled.

00:05:02.967 --> 00:05:07.158
As I saw the swirl of the water
whirlpooling down the drain,

00:05:07.158 --> 00:05:10.350
I figured I could use the same concept

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.899
to suck in waste from 
the surface of the water

00:05:12.899 --> 00:05:15.445
and store it in a chamber.

00:05:15.445 --> 00:05:17.279
So I rushed to the bathtub,

00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:19.598
filled it with water and my toys,

00:05:19.598 --> 00:05:21.417
opened the nozzle,

00:05:21.417 --> 00:05:24.450
and was delighted to see
a whirlpool being created

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:28.169
which started to suck in the water
and toys in the tub

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:31.109
and I immediately knew what I had to do.

00:05:31.109 --> 00:05:34.699
And so I went to my room, 
drew a rough model,

00:05:34.699 --> 00:05:37.789
a circular ship with saucers attached,

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:39.958
a very futuristic design,

00:05:39.958 --> 00:05:43.568
much like the USS Enterprise
from Star Trek -

00:05:43.568 --> 00:05:48.258
we all loved the series, right?

00:05:48.258 --> 00:05:50.701
So I zoomed around the house,

00:05:50.701 --> 00:05:54.319
scrambled and collected the following:

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:56.069
an inflatable tube,

00:05:56.069 --> 00:05:58.628
straws, connection pipes,

00:05:58.628 --> 00:06:00.459
scissors,

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:03.065
some cardboard to make the saucers,

00:06:03.065 --> 00:06:04.609
and voila!

00:06:04.609 --> 00:06:07.218
ERVIS Version 1 was born.

00:06:07.218 --> 00:06:09.412
This was just the first draft.

00:06:09.412 --> 00:06:11.169
The propellers weren't automated

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:17.003
and it only lasted seven seconds
in water before coming apart,

00:06:17.003 --> 00:06:18.727
but there it was,

00:06:18.727 --> 00:06:23.444
my ERVIS, 
my vision of changing the oceans.

00:06:23.444 --> 00:06:25.433
I then got a little more serious,

00:06:25.433 --> 00:06:27.379
did some exhaustive research,

00:06:27.379 --> 00:06:32.604
and realized that a circular shape
was not very efficient,

00:06:32.604 --> 00:06:37.297
so I redesigned it 
to be more stretched with curves.

00:06:37.297 --> 00:06:42.899
I worked with a 3D designer
to make the new design and model of ERVIS.

00:06:42.899 --> 00:06:46.937
Another interesting fact I came across
during my research

00:06:46.937 --> 00:06:50.897
was that large ships like the one you see 
in the picture

00:06:50.897 --> 00:06:55.061
are actually one of the 
biggest pollutants on Earth.

00:06:55.061 --> 00:06:59.549
They use high-sulfur fuel,
which creates a lot of air pollution.

00:06:59.549 --> 00:07:03.521
I wanted to make sure ERVIS didn't
become part of that problem,

00:07:03.521 --> 00:07:08.157
so I looked at renewable energy,
like solar and wind,

00:07:08.157 --> 00:07:11.809
and cleaner ones like hydrogen
and RNG.

00:07:11.809 --> 00:07:15.658
I investigated into using
lighter materials, like graphene,

00:07:15.658 --> 00:07:18.243
which is as durable as steel,

00:07:18.243 --> 00:07:22.042
and making it autonomous, like Tesla.

00:07:22.042 --> 00:07:26.148
I eventually want to see multiple 
ERVISes manning the ocean,

00:07:26.148 --> 00:07:28.946
cleaning and scavenging the waste.

00:07:28.946 --> 00:07:32.168
So, what do I want ERVIS to do?

00:07:32.168 --> 00:07:36.197
As I was doing my research, going through
a variety of resources,

00:07:36.197 --> 00:07:41.076
and after guzzling down innumerable
cups of hot chocolate-

00:07:41.076 --> 00:07:46.018
hey, I'm still a kid, no coffee for me-

00:07:46.018 --> 00:07:51.108
I realized part of the problem
was that we actually don't know

00:07:51.108 --> 00:07:54.074
a lot about the waste that pollutes
our oceans,

00:07:54.074 --> 00:07:57.506
and so I started zeroing down
on the following things

00:07:57.506 --> 00:08:00.556
I really want ERVIS to do:

00:08:00.556 --> 00:08:04.498
the first, to clean the current waste
floating in the ocean,

00:08:04.498 --> 00:08:09.586
second, to analyze the data
from the waste which we collect,

00:08:09.586 --> 00:08:15.299
and the third, stopped at source,
which is getting disposed by the ships.

00:08:15.299 --> 00:08:17.255
So here's how it works.

00:08:17.255 --> 00:08:19.226
The ship is essentially a large boat

00:08:19.226 --> 00:08:23.508
with various compartments 
and saucers surrounding it.

00:08:23.508 --> 00:08:28.519
The working of waste collection
is based on some basic physics concepts,

00:08:28.519 --> 00:08:32.708
like saucers which rotate 
to capture waste,

00:08:32.708 --> 00:08:36.768
a multi-stage cleaner
which is a size-based separation,

00:08:36.768 --> 00:08:40.060
and a compactor to compact the trash.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:44.997
The saucers float on the surface
gravitate to create a whirlpool

00:08:44.997 --> 00:08:47.457
to pull the waste towards its center.

00:08:47.457 --> 00:08:51.497
These saucers will have a central outlet
which will swallow the waste

00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:55.957
and is connected through a tube
to various chambers in the ship.

00:08:55.957 --> 00:08:57.948
Imagine a gigantic vacuum cleaner

00:08:57.948 --> 00:09:02.709
with many cleaning tubes
attached to many dust bags.

00:09:02.709 --> 00:09:04.327
Once the waste enters the tube,

00:09:04.327 --> 00:09:08.387
we'll have various stages, 
which will then segregate it.

00:09:08.387 --> 00:09:12.887
First, we have an infrared sensor,
which alerts ERVIS for marine life.

00:09:12.887 --> 00:09:17.089
The waste then goes to various chambers
for processing.

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:22.839
The first chamber is the oil chamber,
which collects waste oil.

00:09:22.839 --> 00:09:26.788
The second, third, fourth, 
and fifth chambers

00:09:26.788 --> 00:09:32.285
are for large, medium, small
and micro-waste respectively.

00:09:32.285 --> 00:09:34.406
Once the waste enters those chambers,

00:09:34.406 --> 00:09:39.044
we analyze, segregate,
and compact it,

00:09:39.044 --> 00:09:43.384
and pump the filtered water 
back into the oceans.

00:09:43.384 --> 00:09:47.924
Beyond ERVIS, I see myself tackling
other ocean problems,

00:09:47.924 --> 00:09:50.885
like the waste which lies 
at the bottom of the sea,

00:09:50.885 --> 00:09:54.667
and the waste which lies on beaches.

00:09:54.667 --> 00:09:57.394
I would like to do more research on

00:09:57.394 --> 00:10:01.635
how I can help effectively dispose
of the waste -

00:10:01.635 --> 00:10:04.525
maybe process it on the ship itself-

00:10:04.525 --> 00:10:10.376
or creating different storage stations
at the oceans for holding the waste.

00:10:10.376 --> 00:10:12.495
Collector ships can bring the waste there

00:10:12.495 --> 00:10:16.026
and transport ships 
can take them back to land.

00:10:16.026 --> 00:10:20.208
Someone asked me how confident
I was about the success of ERVIS.

00:10:20.208 --> 00:10:22.536
As Chris Anderson wonderfully put it,

00:10:22.536 --> 00:10:27.895
this idea exists and will always 
remain a part of me,

00:10:27.895 --> 00:10:31.024
and nobody can take that away.

00:10:31.024 --> 00:10:36.684
Before I end, I want this to be
a new beginning of hope

00:10:36.684 --> 00:10:39.895
that one day I will see 
my creation in action,

00:10:39.895 --> 00:10:41.665
going there,

00:10:41.665 --> 00:10:43.544
cleaning the oceans,

00:10:43.544 --> 00:10:47.504
and restoring the natural habitat
of our marine world.

00:10:47.504 --> 00:10:48.764
Thank you.

